.TH CONFIGSYS 1 "" "configsys" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
configsys \- Install and manage the software on each of your machines from one config
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B configsys
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIcommand\fR] [\fIargs\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Install and manage the software on each of your machines from one config.
Each machine installs its own picks (the \fBpicks:\fR section of your config) through this OS's
package manager (apt, dnf, pacman, zypper, apk, xbps, brew) or flatpak, snap, AppImages,
tarballs, source builds, language toolchains and more. Profiles are read-only lists to browse
and pick from. With no command, the interactive TUI opens.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB-h\fR, \fB--help\fR
show this help message and exit
.TP
\fB--pretend\fR
dry run: print the commands an operation would run instead of running them
.TP
\fB--os\fR \fIBLOCK\fR
use this OS block instead of the detected one (e.g. pop_os!, fedora, arch); same as CONFIGSYS_OS
.TP
\fB--home\fR \fIDIR\fR
use DIR in place of $HOME for every path configsys reads or writes (config, state, user-scope installs), for sandboxed runs; same as CONFIGSYS_HOME
.TP
\fB--machine\fR \fINAME\fR
work on machine NAME's picks for this run instead of this box's \fBmachine:\fR (to view or curate another machine from here); installs still happen on this box
.TP
\fB--config\fR \fIFILE\fR
your user config file (default ~/.config/configsys/configsys.hu); same as CONFIGSYS_CONFIG
.TP
\fB--color\fR
cap the TUI color depth (default auto: detect it). Lower values only clamp down, 24bit forces direct RGB even without COLORTERM, 16 uses the theme's \fBcolors-basic\fR ANSI set and 8 only the base eight; CONFIGSYS_COLOR sets the same, and NO_COLOR forces none
.TP
\fB--nocolor\fR, \fB--no-color\fR
monochrome TUI, the same as --color none: no colors or gradient, and only the plain built-in startup progress bar; reverse video and bold still mark the cursor and headers
.TP
\fB--effects\fR
TUI motion: full (background gradient + any splash), reduced (no gradient, a calmer splash), none (no gradient, only the plain built-in progress bar). Overrides the \fBeffects:\fR setting; unset picks reduced over SSH, else full
.TP
\fB--probe\fR
print the TUI color mode and motion level this terminal gets, and why, then exit (honors --color, --nocolor and --effects)
.TP
\fB--splash-linger\fR
always show the startup splash, even on a fast load, and keep it animating until you press a key; same as CONFIGSYS_SPLASH=linger
.TP
\fB-v\fR, \fB--verbose\fR
print load detail to stderr: -v adds the layer stack, route overrides and per-unit state; -vv adds each component's winning binding and why (errors and progress always show)
.TP
\fB-q\fR, \fB--silent\fR
print nothing to the console while loading, not even errors (they still appear on the TUI \fB!\fR page and in \fBinspect\fR's issue list)
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBinspect\fR
show the install state of this machine's picks (status, installed and latest versions) and any issues
.TP
\fBinstall\fR
Install components. A name is a component; profile:<name> expands to that profile's components. Components already present are not skipped: a package manager re-runs (a no-op when current), and a \fBvia: source\fR component rebuilds from a clean checkout, so re-running install picks up a recipe change even at the same version. --no-deps acts on only the named components, without installing their dependencies. --force lets a dotfiles install overwrite an on-system file that was never captured (it is backed up to .pre-configsys first). Installing does not add a component to this machine's picks; \fBconfigsys picks add\fR does that. --picks installs every pick of this box that is not installed (with any names given), after showing the plan and asking (--yes skips the question); picks with no install method here are skipped with a notice.
.TP
\fBremove\fR
Uninstall components. A name is a component; profile:<name> expands to that profile's components. Glue that only the removed components needed goes with them; their dependencies stay. A component with no install method left on this OS is still removed when a stranded install of it remains, through the method that made it. Removing does not unpick a component; \fBconfigsys picks rm\fR does that.
.TP
\fBupgrade\fR
Upgrade components to their latest version, or the whole system with --system. A name is a component; profile:<name> expands to that profile's components. Components already present are not skipped: a package manager re-runs (a no-op when current), and a \fBvia: source\fR component rebuilds from a clean checkout, so re-running install picks up a recipe change even at the same version. --no-deps acts on only the named components, without installing their dependencies. --force lets a dotfiles install overwrite an on-system file that was never captured (it is backed up to .pre-configsys first). --picks upgrades every outdated pick of this box (with any names given), after showing the plan and asking (--yes skips the question). --system upgrades the whole machine instead and takes no names; see \fBconfigsys updates\fR.
.TP
\fBlock\fR
Hold components at their installed version. A name is a component; profile:<name> expands to that profile's components. The hold is the package manager's own where it has one (apt-mark hold, dnf versionlock, …), else a record in configsys' ledger; managers that cannot hold a version, such as pacman and apk, decline.
.TP
\fBunlock\fR
Release the version hold on components. A name is a component; profile:<name> expands to that profile's components.
.TP
\fBfix-scope\fR
move installs whose scope (user / system) differs from the configured scope, so they match the config (the config is not changed)
.TP
\fBset-version\fR
install a specific version of a component, where its install method can target one
.TP
\fBmachine\fR
list, add, remove or switch machines (the columns of \fBpicks:\fR)
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBlist\fR, \fBshow\fR, \fBadd\fR, \fBrm\fR, \fBuse\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBdisp\fR
view or set dispositions: your seen / interesting triage marks on catalog components (they never install anything)
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBlist\fR, \fBget\fR, \fBset\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBpicks\fR
list or edit a machine's picks: the components it installs (also editable in the TUI Profiles screen)
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBlist\fR, \fBadd\fR, \fBrm\fR, \fBto-primary\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBwhere\fR
explain a component: source layer, bindings, and how it resolves on this machine
.TP
\fBlocation\fR
print a component's absolute install location as it resolves here (honoring scope, pins and \fBlocations:\fR), for shell snippets
.TP
\fBversions\fR
show the version each install method would install for a component (native vs tarball/source/…), and which lag the newest
.TP
\fBcheck\fR
lint the merged config (repo, plugins, your config and its includes) without installing anything
.TP
\fBpin\fR
view or change install-method / provider pins (local, or portable via a primary plugin)
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBlist\fR, \fBset\fR, \fBunset\fR, \fBpromote\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBprofile\fR
browse profiles: read-only component lists shipped by the repo and plugins (installs come from picks)
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBlist\fR, \fBshow\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBconfig\fR
view or edit machine settings (\fBconfig show\fR lists them all)
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBshow\fR, \fBget\fR, \fBset\fR, \fBunset\fR, \fBmove\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBtheme\fR
view or edit the TUI theme; save/load shareable themes
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBshow\fR, \fBlist\fR, \fBset\fR, \fBunset\fR, \fBsave\fR, \fBload\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBplugin\fR
manage plugins: declare and sync them, bless or init your primary plugin, trust plugins that run code
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBlist\fR, \fBsync\fR, \fBbless\fR, \fBunbless\fR, \fBadd\fR, \fBremove\fR, \fBupdate\fR, \fBtrust\fR, \fBuntrust\fR, \fBinit\fR, \fBset-source\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBreport\fR
assemble an install-failure report (OS + route + driver output) and file it upstream — you approve the full text first
.TP
\fBrequest\fR
ask upstream to support a component on more platforms — shows a coverage matrix of where it installs today; you approve before filing
.TP
\fBrequest-os\fR
suggest THIS OS for inclusion, or report that a system update broke it — captures os-release; you approve before filing
.TP
\fBorphans\fR
list installed software this machine's picks don't account for; adopt (track), remove or ignore it
.TP
\fBdotfiles\fR
show and capture your dotfiles; review glue the shell-writes guard staged
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBstatus\fR, \fBcapture\fR, \fBstaged\fR, \fBactivate\fR, \fBdiscard\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBglue\fR
add your own shell-startup snippets (aliases / env / functions), or override a component's shipped glue; kept in your primary plugin when you have one
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBadd\fR, \fBoverride\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBmanpages\fR
install or check the man pages (configsys(1) + configsys.hu(5))
.RS
.PP
Subcommands: \fBinstall\fR, \fBstatus\fR.
.RE
.TP
\fBupdates\fR
list System Updates: pending updates from this OS's package manager, flatpak and snap for packages that aren't configsys components, grouped by tier; apply with \fBupgrade --system\fR
.TP
\fBrefresh\fR
re-check upstream versions for this machine's picks and refresh the OS package index (sudo may prompt; on Arch it offers a full \fBpacman -Syu\fR instead)
.TP
\fBshow\fR
print the shipped routes.hu or config.hu template, or its path
.TP
\fBkeys\fR
show the effective TUI keybindings (the merged \fBkeys:\fR section)
.TP
\fBtui\fR
open the interactive TUI (the default; prints \fBinspect\fR output when not on a terminal)
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
  configsys                            open the interactive TUI (the default)
  configsys --machine laptop           open the TUI on the `laptop` machine's picks, to curate them
  configsys inspect                    install state of this machine's picks, plus any issues
  configsys picks add ripgrep          track ripgrep: add it to this machine's picks
  configsys install rust ripgrep       install components now (dependencies pulled in and ordered)
  configsys install profile:finders    install every component in the `finders` profile
  configsys upgrade profile:finders btop
                                       upgrade a profile's components plus one more, to latest
  configsys updates                    list System Updates (pending updates outside configsys)
  configsys upgrade --system           preview, confirm, then run each package manager's bulk upgrade
  configsys where neovim               explain how a component resolves on this machine
  configsys location vulkan-sdk        print its absolute install dir (for shell snippets)
  configsys pin set steam flatpak      choose an install method (a local pin)
  configsys pin promote steam          make that pin portable via your primary plugin
  configsys glue add aliases           write your own shell startup snippet in $EDITOR
  configsys show routes                print the shipped routes.hu (--path prints its location)
  configsys --pretend install steam    dry run: print the commands instead of running them
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.nf
  CONFIGSYS_OS, CONFIGSYS_OS_VERSION   use this OS block / version instead of the detected one (--os)
  CONFIGSYS_ARCH                       CPU arch for `$ARCH` and `cpu:` conditions (default: detected)
  CONFIGSYS_HOME                       stand-in for $HOME on every configsys path (--home)
  CONFIGSYS_CONFIG                     your user config file (--config)
  CONFIGSYS_STATE_DIR                  holds your user config, the ledger, caches and synced plugins
                                       (default $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/configsys, else ~/.config/configsys)
  CONFIGSYS_REPO                       data root holding the shipped routes.hu and config.hu
  CONFIGSYS_USERSCOPE_DIR              base dir for user-scope installs (default ~)
  CONFIGSYS_SYSTEMSCOPE_DIR            base dir for system-scope installs (default /opt)
  CONFIGSYS_APP_DIR, CONFIGSYS_SDK_DIR, CONFIGSYS_SRC_DIR
                                       app / SDK / source-tree dirs under that base (default apps,
                                       sdks, src); these five override the `dirs:` setting
  CONFIGSYS_COLOR                      TUI color cap, same values as --color (NO_COLOR forces none)
  CONFIGSYS_EFFECTS                    TUI motion: full, reduced or none (--effects)
  CONFIGSYS_SPLASH                     always = show the startup splash even on a fast load;
                                       linger = also hold it until a key (--splash-linger)
  CONFIGSYS_NO_SPLASH                  set to anything to turn the startup splash off
  CONFIGSYS_GLUE_SHELLS                comma-separated shells to set up glue for (default: detected)
  CONFIGSYS_PM                         apt or dnf: the package manager the gcc / clang drivers use
  CONFIGSYS_GITHUB_TOKEN               GitHub API token: lifts the API rate limit for version checks
                                       (GITHUB_TOKEN also works)
  CONFIGSYS_GIT_TOKEN                  token for cloning private github: / gitlab: plugin repos
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB~/.config/configsys/configsys.hu\fR
per-machine config (profiles, pins, plugins).
.TP
\fB~/.config/configsys/dotfiles/\fR
machine-local dotfiles content store (see dotfiles capture).
.TP
\fB~/.config/configsys/plugins/\fR
synced plugin repos (one dir per plugin).
.TP
\fB~/.config/configsys/plugin-trust.hu\fR
approved content hashes for code plugins.
.TP
\fB~/.config/configsys/state.hu\fR
ledger: version-lock intent + bookkeeping.
.TP
\fB~/.config/configsys/versions.hu\fR
discovered-version cache (24h TTL).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR configsys.hu (5)
